A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 4 \\ 1 & 3 \end{bmatrix}, B = \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 1 \\ 2 & 4 \end{bmatrix}

The transpose of A is A^T = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 4 & 3 \end{bmatrix} and the transpose of B is B^T = \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 2 \\ 1 & 4 \end{bmatrix}

(A B)^T = \begin{bmatrix} 6 & 10 \\ 8 & 12 \end{bmatrix} and (B^T A^T)^T = \begin{bmatrix} 6 & 10 \\ 8 & 12 \end{bmatrix} so (A B)^T = (B^T A^T)^T

The transpose of (A B) is equal to the transpose of (B^T A^T) because the order of multiplication does not change when taking the transpose.

So, (A B)^T = A^T B^T or B^T A^T.

